  "warnings": [
    "Pots: the LTspice model draws each 10 kΩ pot as a 1 Ω and a 10 kΩ resistor in series with one end left open (R3+R4, R9+R10, R13+R14, R17+R18). On the board each pair is one 10 kΩ trimmer, RV1–RV4: pin 1 and the wiper are used, pin 3 stays unconnected. Each knob moves its band's corner frequency.",
    "Changed from the schematic: the D1–D4 bridge has both its AC-return corner and its − output corner on ground. That shorts D3, leaves D4 idle, and makes D1 clamp U8's output at −0.7 V on every negative half-cycle, holding U8 in current limit. Only D2 does useful work, so the board uses D2 alone — a half-wave peak detector into C4 and R1.",
    "Op-amps: eight sections fill two OP484 quads. The 3.375 V LED threshold needs rail-to-rail outputs: a TL074 on ±4.5 V tops out near ±3 V, and the LED would never light.",
    "Speaker: an op-amp output can't drive an 8 Ω speaker. Use headphones through a 100 Ω resistor, or a powered speaker or LM386 amplifier.",
    "V6 (AC 1) isn't connected to anything on the schematic — it's left over from an AC sweep — so it isn't built."
  ],
  "layout": {
    "note": "Laid out by hand: each band's filter sits beside its buffer, the three 100 kΩ summing resistors meet at one strip, and every jumper runs straight along a row or down a column. The app still checked every hole, added the supply drops and proved the board matches the circuit.",
